@* The one place the app explains itself, behind a quiet button in the app bar. It is an HTML
   popover and not a MudDialog because the layout is static and the editor page runs in
   WebAssembly, so there is no interactive island here to open a dialog from. The popover
   attribute gives a top layer panel with a backdrop and light dismiss without any script. *@

<button type="button" class="about-trigger" popovertarget="about">why this exists</button>

<div id="about" class="about" popover>
    <h2>Why this exists</h2>
    <p>
        This is a showcase, not a product. It comes from running user outreach and loyalty tooling,
        where the recurring problems were not knowing who a campaign would reach until it had been
        sent, sending the same message twice, and nobody being able to say what was delivered when
        a provider failed mid-send. It is built to show what a Blazor Web App looks like when those
        parts are done properly. Nothing here comes from any real system.
    </p>

    <h3>What this is</h3>
    <p>
        A campaign builder for a loyalty and rewards product. You build an audience rule, write a
        push and an email variant, schedule the send, approve it and watch it go out through mock
        providers that can be made to fail. Five processes under one Aspire AppHost, Postgres, two
        workers and four providers.
    </p>

    <h3>How to use it</h3>
    <p>
        Open a campaign and edit its rule. The count, the breakdowns and the sample of ten update on
        every change. Click a sampled user for the clauses that matched it. Submit, approve and
        schedule, then open Live and flip <code>push-a</code> down on the Providers page while the
        send runs. The results page groups every skip by reason. All data is synthetic, generated
        from a seed.
    </p>

    <h3>Why the count in the browser is the count on the server</h3>
    <p>
        The rule engine is one assembly with no package references, loaded by the browser in
        WebAssembly and by the web server and the worker. The editor evaluates the rule over all
        20,000 users locally, so the estimate needs no round trip, and the same code decides the
        real audience at schedule time. The chip in the editor says where the evaluation ran. An
        end to end test asserts that both sides return the same count for the same rule.
    </p>

    <h3>Why a user gets one message</h3>
    <p>
        Scheduling writes one delivery row per user per channel in the same transaction as the
        status change, and the table has a unique key on campaign, user and channel. Workers claim
        rows with <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code>, so adding workers changes throughput and not
        the count. A test runs six workers against a provider that fails one call in ten and asserts
        that 3,000 rows produce exactly 3,000 successful calls.
    </p>

    <h3>Why the guardrails run in the worker</h3>
    <p>
        Consent, the suppression list, the frequency cap and quiet hours are checked when the row
        is claimed, not when the campaign is authored, because a user can opt out between approval
        and send. Every skip is written with a reason. Every delivery is one trace with the provider
        calls as children, so a failed primary and the retry through the secondary are visible in
        the Aspire dashboard.
    </p>

    <button type="button" class="about-close" popovertarget="about" popovertargetaction="hide">close</button>
</div>
